/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  aminoBuffers01_tris_glycine -- the Tris and triethanolamine buffers at half neutralisation, and
  a glycine titration both ways: 0.1 mol/kg glycine with 0.5 equivalent of HCl, alone, and with 0.5 and
  1 equivalent of NaOH, at 25 C.  Six beakers, the pH a RESULT of the charge balance, read against
  the evaluated pK's of Goldberg, Kishore & Lennen, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 31 (2002) 231,
  Section 8 (p. 359-360).

  WHAT THIS CASE IS.  A STRUCTURAL ILLUSTRATION of the C373 phase 3 records (amine and amino-acid
  ladders, written by bin/curate/species_c373.py from data/curation/batches/species_c373).  It is
  NOT validated against a measured pH: no work in hand prints a pH for these solutions, so there
  are no `anchor` rows.  The master of each family is the compound AS WEIGHED -- Tris, TEOA and the
  glycine zwitterion HGly -- so the case declares it directly and HCl (the chloride) or NaOH is the
  titrant (`totalsBasis stoichiometric`); `pH solve;` closes the charge balance.  Tris: pK 8.072, dH
  47.45 kJ/mol; triethanolamine: pK 7.762, dH 33.6 (Section 7.68 p. 357, 7.66 p. 353); glycine:
  pK 2.351, 9.780 (Section 7.31 p. 281).

  WHAT TO LOOK FOR.  (this run's numbers, Davies, 25 C)
   1. Tris half-neutralised with HCl (0.025 + 0.025 mol/kg, I = 0.025): pH 8.138 = pK 8.072 +
      log10(1/0.8594), the cation's activity coefficient 0.8594 against 1 for the neutral base;
      triethanolamine the same: 7.828 = 7.762 + 0.066.  A buffer's pH is its pK only at zero ionic
      strength.
   2. Glycine alone (the zwitterion): pH 6.075 against (pK1 + pK2)/2 = 6.066, the isoelectric
      point; HGly is 0.09996 of the 0.1 mol/kg, H2Gly+ and Gly- 1.9e-5 and 2.0e-5.
   3. With 0.5 equivalent of HCl the first step is not a clean half: pH 2.504 against pK1 2.351,
      because the free H+ (3.8e-3 mol/kg) is not negligible beside the 0.1 mol/kg acid (H2Gly+ 0.0461,
      HGly 0.0539).  With 0.5 equivalent of NaOH (Gly- 0.0499, HGly 0.0501) pH 9.693 = pK2 9.780 +
      log10(0.8212) (gamma 0.8212 of the anion); with 1 equivalent the anion is 0.0976 of 0.1 and
      pH 11.278.
   4. No ion pairing with sodium for these anions; the HCl ion pair of the catalogue is the only one
      present (HClaq, 1e-4 or less): the distribution is complete only up to the reactions
      somebody wrote down.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    { name tris_half; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { Tris 0.05 mol/kg; Cl 0.025 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( HTris Tris ); output { file tris_half.csv; } }
    { name triethanolamine_half; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { TEOA 0.05 mol/kg; Cl 0.025 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( HTEOA TEOA ); output { file triethanolamine_half.csv; } }
    { name glycine_HCl0p050; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { HGly 0.10 mol/kg; Cl 0.05 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H2Gly HGly Gly ); output { file glycine_HCl0p050.csv; } }
    { name glycine_zwitterion; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { HGly 0.10 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H2Gly HGly Gly ); output { file glycine_zwitterion.csv; } }
    { name glycine_Na0p050; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { HGly 0.10 mol/kg; Na 0.05 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H2Gly HGly Gly ); output { file glycine_Na0p050.csv; } }
    { name glycine_Na0p100; type speciate; activityModel davies; analyticalTotals { HGly 0.10 mol/kg; Na 0.10 mol/kg; } totalsBasis stoichiometric; pH solve; diagSpecies ( H2Gly HGly Gly ); output { file glycine_Na0p100.csv; } }
);
